// The mirror of `trait_impl_must_be_pub`: inside a `trait`'s OWN declaration, `pub` is refused
// (E0053). Every method a trait declares is already its public contract, so the word would restate
// what `trait` said — and worse, it would suggest the unmarked ones are private, which is the one
// thing it must not imply. Refused rather than accepted-and-ignored, on the same ground `static` is
// refused where a body already decides: a modifier that means nothing is still a modifier a reader
// has to interpret.
//
// It PARSES — `pub` is part of the method production, not a field declaration's opening — so this
// is a diagnostic with a span on the method, not a parse fumble about an unexpected token.
// expect: error E0053 at 14:12
// expect: error E0053 at 17:19
// expect: exit 1
trait Speaks {
    pub fn speak(): string
    // `pub` is read ahead of `static` and the two are judged independently: `static` is legal here
    // (a trait's contract is the one place receiver-ness is declared), `pub` is not.
    pub static fn tag(): string
    fn count(): int
}

echo "unreached";
